No immediate tactics jump out; the position will be decided by its long-term features.

White has the healthier pawn structure. Black's d5-pawn is isolated. Meanwhile, White has the superior minor pieces. White owns the bishop pair in an open position.

A good plan for White: blockade the weak pawn, then pile up on it (key squares: d4 and d5). Another idea for White: look for ways to open the position.
